requirements.detail:
Refinery effluents must be segregated and classified into five distinct categories based on their contamination sources:
a) Effluent free from oil and dissolved organic material (originating from boiler blow-down, water treatment plants, and oil-free storm areas);
b) Effluent accidentally contaminated with oil (originating from tank farms and cooling tower blow-downs);\c) Effluent continuously contaminated with oil but free from other soluble organic material (originating from oil processing areas);
d) Process effluent (originating from desalters, steam stripping condensates, pump gland cooling, barometric condensers containing emulsions, and wash water);
e) Sanitary and domestic effluents (originating from toilets and canteens).

Biological treatment processes used to eliminate biodegradable organic matter and toxic substances (like phenols) are restricted by strict incoming oil concentration limits:
a) Trickling filters can tolerate an incoming oil concentration of up to 100 ppm;
b) Activated sludge processes can tolerate an incoming oil concentration of up to 25 ppm;
c) Oxidation ponds cannot tolerate high oil concentrations, as surface oil films retard essential atmospheric reaeration and photosynthesis.

Chemical methods to remove emulsified oil, settle suspended solids, and eliminate toxic substances are categorized into:
a) Neutralization: Neutralizes acidic or alkaline wastes (e.g., diluting acid/alkali, neutralizing spent caustic with spent acid or gas, neutralizing sulphuric sludges or hydrofluoric acid wastes); ) Precipitation and Clarification: Precipitates specific refinery compounds (such as spent caustic with acidic oils, sulphide water with iron salts, sulphonates with lime, or cooling-water chromates with ferrous sulphate and lime);\c) Chemical Oxidation: Applied specifically to destroy toxic phenols and cyanides in waste, or for waste combustion;
d) Regeneration: Regenerates chemical absorbents (like monoethanolamine used to absorb hydrogen sulphide and mercaptans) using steam stripping.
